description:
rsQuat::slerp handles three cases by the cosine of the angle between the inputs. The third -- when the quaternions are very nearly opposite, so 1 + cn <= RS_EPSILON -- is broken.

    scalea = sinf((1.0f - t) * RS_PIo2);
    scaleb = sinf(t * RS_PIo2);
    q[0] = scalea * a[0] - scaleb * q[1];
    q[1] = scalea * a[1] + scaleb * q[0];
    q[2] = scalea * a[2] - scaleb * q[3];
    q[3] = scalea * a[3] + scaleb * q[2];

Two problems in four lines. It reads q[1], q[3] and q[2] -- the destination quaternion's own prior contents, which are whatever the caller happened to leave there rather than anything meaningful. And q[0] and q[2] are read on the line after they are written, so two of the four terms use the value just computed rather than the old one.

The intent was presumably to interpolate towards some perpendicular quaternion, since the great circle between antipodal rotations is undefined and any perpendicular will do. What it actually produces depends on the destination's prior state and on statement order.

Deliberately NOT covered by a test. A case here would pin garbage: the output is a function of uninitialised-in-practice memory, so any assertion would either encode one particular caller's leftovers or be too loose to fail. Fixing it needs a decision about what the antipodal case should return, and the test comes with the fix.

## Steps to Reproduce

Build two quaternions that are nearly opposite (cn close to -1), slerp between them from two different destination quaternions, and observe that the results differ.
